  so i'm just going to write these into my  table so  this one is zero then one two  three and four  so that basically is the experiment you  need to do  ideally you'd like to do at least six  different masses but i'm just going to  show  using four for now okay  so what i'm going to do now is i'm going  to show you  what the graph looks like that  accompanies this because we need to be  able to understand a couple of features  from this graph  now the first very unusual thing about  these elasticity graphs  is the fact that the independent  variable  actually goes on the y-axis normally the  independent variable  on pretty much every other graph we ever  do  the the independent variable goes on the  x-axis but there is an exception  when doing these extension graphs and  one of the reasons  behind that is the fact that  these graphs can be used to find the  spring constant  and it's slightly easier to find the  spring constant when  it's drawn this way round so that's  why the graph looks a little bit weird  because the independent variable  for a change is on the y axis okay  so this is extension  in centimeters here now  what we should find when we plot one of  these graphs  is obviously i'm not using a ruler or  graph paper here i'm just doing it free  hand  but hopefully we get the idea that we  should have  a nice linear relationship  to begin with so we should have a nice  straight line  here obviously i haven't been able to  draw it perfectly straight because i  haven't got a ruler  um just drawing freehand today but we  should have a nice straight line  now if we continued this experiment  so we kept on adding more and more force  we would find  two other things we would eventually get  to this particular point  which is called the limit  of proportionality  now what that means is is after this  point  we've added so many masses there's so  much force rather  that we no longer have a linear  relationship so it's no longer directly  proportional  that is called the limit of  proportionality now if we keep going a  little bit  further we get to another point  which is called the elastic  limit now what the elastic limit is  is when you've added so much force  that the spring can no longer return to  its original  shape it's said to be inelastically  distorted  so after that point the spring will not  return  to its original shape it is now  inelastically distorted  so that's an experiment to investigate  the force  force effect on extension this is what  the graph looks like remember it's a bit  unusual because the independent variable  the force  is on the y-axis remember we start off  with a nice  linear relationship so a nice straight  line then we get to the limit  proportionality  and the elastic limit if we kept on  adding more and more force to the  springs  thank you
 

